A construction-aware check found a real flaw in the existing draft: because 12 Walsh frequencies were omitted, guessing a random Walsh character solved about 9% of shipping instances (89/1000), and 32 guesses solved 975/1000. I’m replacing that weak planting with an affine-block completion: the missing Fourier block has 32 degrees of freedom, and 32 pinned coordinates encode a random balanced Boolean quotient function. This keeps the witness a native sign vector, makes the generic exact solver a measured \(O(n^3)\) linear solve, and leaves a sub-300-operation Walsh/Fourier shortcut.
