codex
Implemented a self-contained Track B generator grounded in the paper’s native seminormalized Hadamard-vector completion problem ([paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1606.09368)).

Deliverables:

- [gen_1606_09368.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1606.09368/gen_1606_09368.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1606.09368/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1606.09368/README.md)
- [bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1606.09368/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1606.09368/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1606.09368/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Local results:

- All G1–G9 local gates pass.
- Structure-aware guesses: 0/200,000.
- Seven attacks: each 0/8.
- Reference Gauss–Jordan solver: 8/8, mean 1,018,271 modular operations.
- Compact Walsh route: 64 sign negations.
- Answer: 128 atoms, 321 characters.
- Canonical-key tests: 160/160 invariant, 20/20 unrelated instances distinct.

The required oracle hardening remains externally blocked: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” before any model produced a scorable response in all three runs. The transcripts record these as API errors, not model failures, so the result is locally verified but cannot honestly claim a `hardened` verdict yet.
